# modules.cpp ePaper support

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other v1.2.0 specifications. This document specifies the
implemented transport and SSD1680 controller plus the first concrete wiring and
provider beneath mm.display. The pico_epaper profile is implemented from the
named Waveshare panel and carrier documentation. A successful build is not a
claim of visual hardware qualification.

docs/modules-display.mdy owns the portable display boundary and platform
binding. docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy owns the implemented MCU provider model.
docs/modules-platform-pico.mdy owns the Pico SDK bridge. This document must not
weaken any of those boundaries to make one panel work quickly.

# Separation of responsibilities

Four layers own four different facts:

- mm.mcu owns reusable GPIO, SPI, and monotonic timing operations.
- A controller module owns the controller command protocol and refresh state.
- A display provider owns panel parameters and fixed physical wiring.
- A board profile states that the provider's display is actually attached.

The Pico SDK adapter does not expose ePaper commands. The controller does not
include Pico SDK headers. The portable application does not know which
controller or GPIOs were selected.

# Implemented foundation

mm.mcu exports typed SPI configuration, span writes, and full-duplex transfer.
The host provider records those calls, and platform.pico.mcu implements them
through the Pico SDK hardware_spi target.

mm.epaper.ssd1680 implements bounded reset and busy handling, descriptor-owned
initialization commands, one-bit packed writes, full refresh, optional partial
refresh, clear, and deep sleep. Host tests exercise command ordering, invalid
descriptors and regions, timeout, bounded streaming, and non-byte-aligned
visible widths. The module contains no Pico SDK header and requires no full
framebuffer.

# MCU SPI facility

The reusable mm.mcu:spi partition is exported by mm.mcu. ePaper is its first
consumer, not part of the transport contract.

The API must support:

- selecting an SPI controller instance;
- configuring clock, transmit, and optional receive GPIOs;
- baud rate, clock polarity, clock phase, and bit order;
- ordered writes of a contiguous byte span;
- optional full-duplex transfer for other future devices;
- explicit status for invalid pins, unsupported instances, and transport
  failure.

Chip select is controlled by the device driver through GPIO because an ePaper
transaction also coordinates data/command and sometimes reset. The SPI layer
must not toggle chip select between command and payload spans behind the
driver's back.

The conceptual configuration is:

Opening fence
struct SpiConfiguration {
    unsigned int instance;
    unsigned int clock_gpio;
    unsigned int transmit_gpio;
    optional<unsigned int> receive_gpio;
    unsigned long baud;
    SpiMode mode;
    BitOrder bit_order;
};
Closing fence

The final types may differ, but configuration and data transfer remain separate
operations. Byte spans are preferred to null-terminated or word-sized buffers.

mm::mcu::Platform gains virtual SPI functions whose default answer is
Unsupported. The host test platform records calls without hardware. The Pico
provider delegates through its existing private C adapter, whose implementation
uses the Pico SDK hardware_spi target. A direct-register platform may implement
SPI later without changing controller modules.

# Controller modules

Each command family is an ordinary globally unique C++ module. Illustrative
layout and names are:

Opening fence
modules/mm/epaper/ssd1680/
    mm.mdy
    ssd1680.cppm
    src/ssd1680.cpp

module: mm.epaper.ssd1680
use: mm.mcu
Closing fence

The first implemented command family is SSD1680. Selecting a physical panel
still requires controller evidence rather than a marketing name: panels with
similar dimensions may use different controller revisions and waveforms.

A controller receives immutable wiring and panel descriptors. The implemented
SSD1680 subset contains:

- SPI instance and configuration;
- chip-select, data/command, reset, and busy GPIOs;
- active levels for reset and busy;
- visible width and height, with X-then-Y RAM traversal;
- the one-bit white/black packed format specified by mm.display;
- reset, busy, and refresh timeouts;
- provider-owned initialization bytes and update controls required by that
  controller revision.

SSD1680 memory dimensions beyond its 176 by 296 limit and coordinate transforms
other than the implemented top-left, X-then-Y traversal require descriptor and
controller extensions before a provider may claim them.

The descriptor is immutable after construction. A controller must validate pin
references against the selected board inventory where that inventory is
available, and must reject duplicate control pins unless the hardware
explicitly permits them.

# Lifecycle and state

The controller owns a visible state machine rather than sending isolated magic
command sequences. At minimum it distinguishes uninitialized, ready,
refreshing, sleeping, and failed states.

Initialization configures SPI and GPIOs, performs the documented hardware reset,
waits for busy with a bounded timeout, identifies or assumes the exact supported
controller revision, and loads required panel settings. No busy wait is
unbounded; delay_ms and ticks_ms provide timeout behavior without controller
knowledge of a vendor timer.

A packed write updates controller memory. Refresh is a separate operation so an
application can update several regions before paying the panel refresh cost.
Full refresh is the baseline. Partial refresh is exposed only when the exact
controller and panel combination has been qualified for it. Unsupported partial
refresh returns a precise status rather than silently performing a full refresh.

Sleep leaves control pins and controller state documented. A subsequent call
either performs a specified wake sequence or requires initialize again. Power
loss, reset during refresh, and timeout recovery must have defined outcomes.

# Pixel data and memory

The controller consumes mm.display's one-bit format directly. X must be byte
aligned. Width must be byte aligned unless the rectangle reaches the visible
right edge; unused low bits in that final byte are outside the visible region.
Rows advance from top to bottom and bytes from left to right.

No layer requires a whole-screen framebuffer. The driver accepts bounded rows,
tiles, or rectangles and streams them through SPI. A higher graphics module may
allocate a framebuffer when the target has enough memory.

Repeated single-byte virtual calls are not an acceptable data path. Command
transitions may be individual operations, but payload bytes cross the MCU seam
in spans so Pico SDK and direct-register providers can use efficient transfers.

# Pico SDK implementation

The Pico adapter adds generic MCU SPI entry points and includes Pico SDK SPI
headers only inside the existing project-owned adapter source. The bridge links
hardware_spi for executables whose selected provider closure needs it. If the
first implementation links hardware_spi lane-wide, that temporary cost must be
recorded and the generic external builder must still contain no ePaper name.

The display provider is a project-owned C++ module with a name such as:

Opening fence
platform.pico_epaper.display
Closing fence

It imports mm.display and the verified controller module, owns the descriptor,
and registers the resulting Display object. It does not use the lib. prefix
unless it is actually a wrapper for a manifest-declared foreign library.

The corresponding composite board profile binds mm.display to that provider and
maps to a real Pico SDK PICO_BOARD value. Standard pico, pico-w, pico2-arm, and
other bare development-board profiles keep no display binding.

# Foreign driver alternative

Using a vendor or community driver is allowed but follows the established
library boundary:

- the checkout is declared as kind: library;
- the project wrapper module begins with lib.;
- only the wrapper receives the foreign include interface;
- project applications import a project interface, not foreign headers;
- the licence and source containment rules remain enforced;
- the selected display provider carries the visible dependency on the wrapper.

A foreign driver does not justify leaking Pico SDK types into mm.display or
adding controller-specific calls to mm::mcu::Platform. A small project-owned
driver is preferred when it makes the command protocol easier to test and audit.

# Test strategy

Controller tests use a fake MCU Platform that records GPIO, SPI, and timer
operations. Tests compare semantic command transcripts rather than merely
checking that a source file compiled.

Required host tests cover:

- SPI configuration and large-span transfer ordering;
- reset sequence and active levels;
- command versus data GPIO transitions;
- initialization transcript for the exact controller revision;
- busy becoming ready and busy timing out;
- rectangle and packed-data validation;
- full refresh;
- partial refresh accepted only when supported;
- sleep and the specified reinitialization path;
- transport errors propagated without continuing the command sequence;
- no controller or provider objects in an unrelated executable closure.

Target qualification builds one portable app through the real Pico external
CMake bridge, validates the ELF and UF2, and confirms exactly one display and
MCU provider registration object. It then flashes through flash.sh.

Hardware verification records the exact development board, carrier or wiring,
panel model and revision, observed controller marking where available, power
voltage, toolchain, Pico SDK, picotool version, and whether each refresh mode was
visually confirmed. A successful link or flash is not reported as a successful
screen refresh.

# Implementation stages

1. Pending hardware identification: record the first physical panel, its
   schematic or wiring, and controller evidence.
2. Implemented: add mm.mcu:spi, host transcript tests, and Pico adapter support.
3. Implemented: add mm.epaper.ssd1680 with initialization and timeout tests.
4. Implemented: add mm.display and a host stand-in independent of ePaper.
5. Implemented: add the pico_epaper composite board and its unique display
   provider for Waveshare Pico-ePaper-2.66.
6. Implemented: add apps/epaper-smoke using only mm.display.
7. Implemented for the profile's inherited Pico ARM platform: build the app
   through the external CMake bridge and publish its ELF and UF2 artifacts.
8. Pending hardware: flash and perform recorded hardware qualification.
9. Future: add partial refresh or further panels only after the baseline full-refresh
   implementation remains covered.

# Acceptance criteria

- Controller host tests pass without Pico SDK or physical hardware.
- SPI payloads cross the platform boundary as spans, not byte-at-a-time virtual
  calls.
- No controller source includes Pico SDK headers.
- No generic external-builder source contains an ePaper controller or panel
  name.
- A standard Pico board selection does not make epaper-smoke available.
- The composite profile makes epaper-smoke available and injects exactly one
  display provider.
- A full refresh displays a deterministic test pattern on the named panel.
- Disconnecting or holding busy produces a bounded timeout rather than a hang.
- Sleep and subsequent documented recovery work on hardware.
- The produced UF2 passes the existing validator and picotool identity check
  before flashing.

# What this does not settle

- SSD1680 is implemented and the Pico-ePaper-2.66 profile fixes one physical
  pinout. Its panel-specific partial-refresh LUT remains unselected, and no
  physical refresh result is claimed before hardware qualification.
- Tri-color, grayscale, and partial-refresh behavior are controller capabilities,
  not promises of the first implementation.
- Temperature compensation and external temperature sensors remain future
  controller policy unless required by the selected panel specification.
- DMA and interrupt-driven SPI are optimizations after the synchronous contract
  is correct.
- Generic sensor enumeration and arbitrary runtime hardware composition are not
  introduced here.
